---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import RegressionDemo from "../components/RegressionDemo.astro";
import LossExplorer from "../components/LossExplorer.astro";
import HpoPath from "../components/HpoPath.astro";
import GradientSlice from "../components/GradientSlice.astro";
import ArdBars from "../components/ArdBars.astro";
import GradientField from "../components/GradientField.astro";

const description =
  "Interactive explainers for support vector machines: regression with the ε-tube, smoothed loss functions, gradient-based hyperparameter optimization, exact hypergradients and automatic relevance determination.";
---

<Base title="Explore: interactive SVM explainers" description={description}>
  <section class="intro">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Explore</p>
      <h1>See the ideas at work</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Small interactive experiments built on the solver and the optimizer of the thesis. The first two run
        the Rust solver compiled to WebAssembly in your browser; the others replay precomputed runs of
        <code>optimized-svm</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="regression">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Regression</p>
      <h2>The ε-tube and sparse solutions</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        Points inside the tube do not contribute to the loss, so the model depends only on the points at
        or beyond its boundary. Draw your own data, widen the tube and watch the support vectors disappear.
      </p>
      <RegressionDemo />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="loss">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Smoothing</p>
      <h2>Why a smoothed loss makes hyperparameters differentiable</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        The hinge and ε-insensitive losses have kinks, so the solution of the training problem is not
        differentiable in the hyperparameters. Replacing <code>max(0, t)</code> by a smooth approximation of
        width δ rounds the kinks; only the few terms inside the band of width 2δ have positive curvature and
        react to a change of the hyperparameters.
      </p>
      <LossExplorer />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="hpo">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Hyperparameter optimization</p>
      <h2>Follow the gradient instead of the grid</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        Because the smoothed solution is differentiable, the cross-validation error has an exact gradient
        with respect to λ and γ. A quasi-Newton method needs a handful of evaluations where a grid needs
        hundreds. Press replay and watch the model improve along the path.
      </p>
      <HpoPath />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="field">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Objective function</p>
      <h2>The cross-validation error and its gradient</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        The hyperparameter objective is the cross-validation error as a function of λ and γ. Every point of
        this map carries an exact gradient, computed without finite differences. Follow the arrows downhill,
        or click to start a gradient descent yourself.
      </p>
      <GradientField />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="gradient">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Hypergradient</p>
      <h2>An exact gradient of the validation error</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        The implicit function theorem turns the optimality conditions of the smoothed training problem into an
        exact derivative of the cross-validation error with respect to the hyperparameters. Move the marker
        along the curve: the tangent always has the slope of the gradient.
      </p>
      <GradientSlice />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="ard">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Many hyperparameters</p>
      <h2>One kernel width per feature</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        Because the cost of the gradient does not grow with the number of hyperparameters, every feature can
        get its own kernel scaling. Irrelevant features are switched off automatically.
      </p>
      <ArdBars />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .intro {
    padding: 3rem 0 1rem;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4vw, 2.7rem);
  }
</style>
